# Creating and Adding Elements

So far we have modified elements that were already on the page because we wrote them in HTML. But how do we add *new* elements?

Technically we could do it by setting `innerHTML`, and as we saw two lessons ago that means hard-coding markup inside a string, with all the security problems that brings. There is a better way, and it takes two steps:

1. **Create** the element with `document.createElement(tagName)`.
2. **Add** it to the DOM tree with a method like `append`.

### A Note About These Cells

Inside a notebook cell, `document` refers to that cell's own output area rather than to the whole page, which means `document.createElement` is not available there. Every runnable cell below therefore starts with one extra line:

```javascript
const document = window.document;
```

That points `document` at the real page document, exactly as it already is in a `<script>` element. **In a real page you do not write that line**; everything after it is what you would actually type.

## The Two Steps

Suppose we have a paragraph and we want to put a `<strong>` element containing the word `world` inside it.

```html id=page1
<h1>Hello</h1>
<p>Hello </p>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const document = window.document;

const p = document.querySelector('p');

const newElem = document.createElement('strong');

console.log(newElem);
```
```+id=page1
```

`document.createElement('strong')` takes the tag name as a string and hands back a new element object. Run that cell and look at the page: **nothing changed.** The element exists, but it lives outside the DOM tree, so there is nothing to see.

Now give it some text and attach it:

```{javascript autorun}
const document = window.document;

const p = document.querySelector('p');

const newElem = document.createElement('strong');
newElem.textContent = 'world';

p.append(newElem);
```
```+id=page1
```

`p.append(newElem)` adds the new element to the tree as the last child of the paragraph, and only then does it render.

That "created but not attached" state is worth getting used to. If you build an element and nothing appears, the missing step is almost always the second one.

## Where the New Element Goes

`append` puts the element last. Four methods together cover everything you normally need. Given a `parent` already in the tree, and a `sibling` already in the tree:

```javascript
parent.append(newEl);    // add newEl as the LAST child of parent
parent.prepend(newEl);   // add newEl as the FIRST child of parent
sibling.after(newEl);    // insert newEl immediately AFTER sibling
sibling.before(newEl);   // insert newEl immediately BEFORE sibling
```

Here they are on one list. Watch where each item lands:

```html id=page2
<ul>
  <li>First</li>
  <li id="second">Second</li>
  <li>Third</li>
</ul>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const document = window.document;

const list = document.querySelector('ul');
const second = document.querySelector('#second');

function makeItem(text) {
  const li = document.createElement('li');
  li.textContent = text;
  return li;
}

list.append(makeItem('appended: last child'));
list.prepend(makeItem('prepended: first child'));
second.after(makeItem('after the second item'));
second.before(makeItem('before the second item'));
```
```+id=page2
```

You will also run into older methods such as `parent.appendChild(newEl)` and `parent.insertBefore(newEl, reference)`. They still work, and these four cover the same ground more readably.

### Strings and Several Arguments

These methods are not limited to a single element. Pass a string and it is added as text. Pass several arguments and they are all added, in order.

```html id=page3
<p>Some </p>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const document = window.document;

const p = document.querySelector('p');

const strongEl = document.createElement('strong');
strongEl.textContent = 'bold';

const emEl = document.createElement('em');
emEl.textContent = 'italic';

p.append(strongEl, ' and ', emEl);
```
```+id=page3
```

That adds an element, then a piece of plain text, then another element, without needing a wrapper around them.

## Building a List From Data

Here is the pattern you will use most. Imagine writing code for a bulletin board: you have an array of comments from somewhere, and you need each one to become a list item on the page.

```html id=page4
<ul id="comments-container"></ul>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const document = window.document;

const list = document.querySelector('#comments-container');
const comments = ['Great post!', 'Thanks for sharing', 'Very helpful'];

let commentNumber = 1;

for (const comment of comments) {
  const li = document.createElement('li');
  li.textContent = comment;
  li.prepend('Comment ' + commentNumber + ': ');

  list.append(li);

  commentNumber++;
}
```
```+id=page4
```

For each string we create an `li`, set its text, prepend a numbered label to the front of that text, and append it to the container. The HTML started with an empty list; everything you see came from the array.

This is the thing that makes a page dynamic: the markup no longer has to know what content it will hold.

## Exercises

1. Create a new list item with the text `Cherry` and add it as the last child of the list.

    ```html id=page5
    <ul id="fruits">
      <li>Apple</li>
      <li>Banana</li>
    </ul>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const document = window.document;

    const fruits = document.querySelector('#fruits');

    // Write your code here
    ```
    ```+id=page5
    ```
    ```+solution
    const document = window.document;

    const fruits = document.querySelector('#fruits');

    const li = document.createElement('li');
    li.textContent = 'Cherry';

    fruits.append(li);
    ```
    ```+test
    assert('#fruits li').should('have.length', 3).run('The list should end up with three items', 'Added a third item');
    assert('#fruits li:last-child').should('have.text', 'Cherry').run('The new `li` should be the last child and read `Cherry`', 'Appended Cherry to the end');
    ```

2. Create a list item with the text `Plan work` and make it the **first** child of the list.

    ```html id=page6
    <ul id="tasks">
      <li>Write code</li>
      <li>Test code</li>
    </ul>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const document = window.document;

    const tasks = document.querySelector('#tasks');

    // Write your code here
    ```
    ```+id=page6
    ```
    ```+solution
    const document = window.document;

    const tasks = document.querySelector('#tasks');

    const li = document.createElement('li');
    li.textContent = 'Plan work';

    tasks.prepend(li);
    ```
    ```+test
    assert('#tasks li').should('have.length', 3).run('The list should end up with three items', 'Added a third item');
    assert('#tasks li:first-child').should('have.text', 'Plan work').run('The new `li` should be the first child. Use `prepend` rather than `append`', 'Prepended the new item');
    ```

3. Insert a new list item with the text `Lansing` immediately after the item with ID `detroit`.

    ```html id=page7
    <ul>
      <li>Ann Arbor</li>
      <li id="detroit">Detroit</li>
      <li>Grand Rapids</li>
    </ul>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const document = window.document;

    const detroit = document.querySelector('#detroit');

    // Write your code here
    ```
    ```+id=page7
    ```
    ```+solution
    const document = window.document;

    const detroit = document.querySelector('#detroit');

    const li = document.createElement('li');
    li.textContent = 'Lansing';

    detroit.after(li);
    ```
    ```+test
    assert('li').should('have.length', 4).run('The list should end up with four items', 'Added an item');
    wrap(document.querySelectorAll('li')[2].textContent).should('equal', 'Lansing').run('`Lansing` should be the third item, immediately after `Detroit`. Use `detroit.after(...)`', 'Inserted it in the right place');
    ```

4. Turn the `cities` array into list items inside the empty list, one `li` per city, in order.

    ```html id=page8
    <ul id="city-list"></ul>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const document = window.document;

    const list = document.querySelector('#city-list');
    const cities = ['Ann Arbor', 'Detroit', 'Lansing'];

    // Write your loop here
    ```
    ```+id=page8
    ```
    ```+solution
    const document = window.document;

    const list = document.querySelector('#city-list');
    const cities = ['Ann Arbor', 'Detroit', 'Lansing'];

    for (const city of cities) {
      const li = document.createElement('li');
      li.textContent = city;
      list.append(li);
    }
    ```
    ```+test
    assert('#city-list li').should('have.length', 3).run('There should be one `li` for each of the three cities', 'Created three list items');
    wrap(document.querySelector('#city-list').textContent).should('equal', 'Ann ArborDetroitLansing').run('The three cities should appear in the same order as the array', 'The cities are in order');
    ```

